1
00:00:08,008 --> 00:00:10,101
Excuse me.

2
00:00:11,678 --> 00:00:14,545
Guy, there's a messenger
outside with a letter,

3
00:00:14,614 --> 00:00:17,549
but he's insisting he gives it to
the lord of the manor in person.

4
00:00:17,617 --> 00:00:20,677
- Is it from Lady Marian?
- No. He says it's from the King.

5
00:00:22,055 --> 00:00:23,818
The King?

6
00:00:26,259 --> 00:00:28,227
So, where is he, then?

7
00:00:28,294 --> 00:00:30,262
Well, he was here a minute ago.

8
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:34,061
He thought Robin was lord of the manor.
Maybe he found out he wasn't and ran off.

9
00:00:34,134 --> 00:00:35,795
What fool told him that?

10
00:00:35,869 --> 00:00:39,202
- It's not a secret, is it?
- Fool!

11
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:41,934
- Get after him. I want that letter.
- Yeah.

12
00:00:42,042 --> 00:00:44,909
He won't get far. He's only on foot. Hah!

13
00:00:45,512 --> 00:00:47,707
Hey, guards, come on.

14
00:00:49,049 --> 00:00:50,880
Happy birthday, Gisborne.

15
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:54,010
- Party going well, is it?
- We've got a problem.

16
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:04,557
- Give us the letter, we'll let you go, all right?
- No!

17
00:01:21,448 --> 00:01:23,416
Over here!

18
00:01:25,585 --> 00:01:28,053
Oi! Get over here!
The tracks are down here!

19
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:29,884
Come along!

20
00:01:29,956 --> 00:01:32,754
You've got the most distinctive tracks
in the forest.

21
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,426
- You might as well give up now.
- This way!

22
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:28,436
- Give us the message from the King.
- It's for Robin of Locksley only.

23
00:02:28,515 --> 00:02:30,710
Just give it to me or we'll take it from you.

24
00:02:39,526 --> 00:02:42,222
Don't you know it's rude
to read other people's mail?

25
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:26,138
By the time you get close enough
to use those swords,

26
00:03:26,206 --> 00:03:29,437
you'll be so full of arrows,
you'll look like hedgehogs.

27
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:38,273
So, this is what
Gisborne's dogsbody does, is it?

28
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:41,081
I'm not his dogsbody.
I'm his right-hand man.

29
00:03:41,154 --> 00:03:42,746
Oh.

30
00:03:43,723 --> 00:03:45,953
I couldn't face myself in the morning
if I was you.

31
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:47,856
I count my money in the morning.

32
00:03:47,927 --> 00:03:50,555
While you two are squabbling,
there's a man in agony.

33
00:03:50,663 --> 00:03:53,029
Run back to your master.

34
00:03:53,099 --> 00:03:54,828
Go on.

35
00:03:55,802 --> 00:03:57,770
Go on!

36
00:03:58,771 --> 00:04:00,705
Run!

37
00:04:00,773 --> 00:04:04,265
- Do you practice little speeches like that?
- Like what?

38
00:04:04,344 --> 00:04:07,040
"You'll be so full of arrows,
you'll look like hedgehogs. "

39
00:04:07,113 --> 00:04:08,512
No. It just came to mind.

40
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,345
- You practice, don't you?
- No.

41
00:04:12,552 --> 00:04:15,248
- Robin...
- Shh... Shh...

42
00:04:15,321 --> 00:04:17,255
I know you.

43
00:04:17,323 --> 00:04:20,349
Save your strength.

44
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:22,428
Lardner.

45
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:26,899
Lardner!

46
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:36,301
It's the royal seal. It is from Richard.

47
00:04:40,346 --> 00:04:43,110
- So, where are they, Gisborne?
- They'll be here.

48
00:04:43,182 --> 00:04:45,707
Woodcock, Sir Guy. Birthday treat.

49
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:50,779
- Shall I get started?
- If you must.

50
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:53,518
Your men had better get to him
before Robin does.

51
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:01,024
He wants reinforcements. He wants more men.

52
00:05:02,001 --> 00:05:05,334
He still has no idea.
He has no idea what's happening in England!

53
00:05:05,405 --> 00:05:08,340
The message you sent with Carter
can't have reached the King yet.

54
00:05:08,408 --> 00:05:12,174
Many a courier get intercepted.
The proof of that's right here.

55
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:16,543
He wants me to send a note back
with... with Lardner,

56
00:05:16,616 --> 00:05:20,143
telling him how many men I can recruit
and when I'll arrive.

57
00:05:20,219 --> 00:05:22,187
Well, you can't do that now, can you?

58
00:05:23,589 --> 00:05:26,183
But... This...

59
00:05:26,292 --> 00:05:28,260
This isn't Lardner. This...

60
00:05:28,328 --> 00:05:30,762
This is McLellan. This is Laurence McLellan.

61
00:05:30,863 --> 00:05:32,831
Well, then, who's Lardner?

62
00:05:38,805 --> 00:05:41,433
Richard the Lion-Heart, they call him.

63
00:05:41,507 --> 00:05:46,843
Now, going into battle in a foreign land,
it's not the heart of a lion you want, is it?

64
00:05:46,913 --> 00:05:51,543
Huh? Since when
did a heart hurt anybody? Huh?

65
00:05:52,418 --> 00:05:54,545
Come on, then! Come on!

66
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,781
I've got the heart of a lion.

67
00:05:59,092 --> 00:06:02,357
You can imagine
the Saracens translating that, can't you?

68
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:06,298
He's got the what of a lion?

69
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:08,561
The teeth?

70
00:06:08,668 --> 00:06:11,432
The jaws? The claws?

71
00:06:13,139 --> 00:06:16,870
No... the heart.

72
00:06:16,943 --> 00:06:21,744
Richard the Lion-Claw, "Jerusalem's yours.
Come in. Sit down. Help yourself. "

73
00:06:22,982 --> 00:06:25,917
Lion-Heart...

74
00:06:26,686 --> 00:06:28,677
- He's good. Very good.
- Forget about it.

75
00:06:28,755 --> 00:06:32,054
It's no wonder
it's taking him so long over there, is it?

76
00:06:36,429 --> 00:06:38,397
Mind you... Mind you...

77
00:06:39,332 --> 00:06:41,698
...it's better than his brother, isn't it?

78
00:06:41,768 --> 00:06:43,929
Prince John.

79
00:06:44,003 --> 00:06:46,028
What's Prince John doing?

80
00:06:46,105 --> 00:06:49,336
Sitting at home. Heart of a dormouse?

81
00:06:49,409 --> 00:06:51,934
Prince John is a friend of mine.

82
00:06:52,011 --> 00:06:56,175
It's a joke. It's what I do. I do other
things. Let's try some soothsaying. Sit down.

83
00:06:56,282 --> 00:07:00,082
Sit down. Sit down. I'll make some
predictions and then I'll make them come true.

84
00:07:00,153 --> 00:07:03,850
Someone in this room will bare their
buttocks to the Sheriff tonight.

85
00:07:03,923 --> 00:07:06,084
Take that man to the dungeons now!

86
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:09,788
Hang on. Prediction two.
Sir Guy's joint will rise from the dead.

87
00:07:09,896 --> 00:07:13,059
- What's that supposed to mean?
- Your woodcock, Sir Guy.

88
00:07:13,132 --> 00:07:15,259
- I can bring it back to life.
- Get rid of him.

89
00:07:15,334 --> 00:07:17,393
My third prediction is about Prince John.

90
00:07:17,503 --> 00:07:20,666
- One day, Prince John will be King of England.
- I know that already.

91
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:23,038
But something in Nottingham can stop him.

92
00:07:23,109 --> 00:07:26,340
- Like what?
- Lardner's ring.

93
00:07:26,412 --> 00:07:30,576
- What is Lardner's ring?
- Let me go and I'll tell you.

94
00:07:30,683 --> 00:07:34,380
Lock him in the cell overnight, flog him
in the morning and then he'll tell us.

95
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:36,445
Now, that really is funny!

96
00:07:39,392 --> 00:07:41,917
You should listen to me.
I know what I'm talking about.

97
00:07:43,196 --> 00:07:44,686
Ah!

98
00:07:44,764 --> 00:07:46,732
The first prediction.

99
00:07:46,799 --> 00:07:51,668
I told you, and the others will follow.
Beware the power of Lardner's ring.

100
00:07:53,372 --> 00:07:55,135
What is Lardner's ring?

101
00:08:03,182 --> 00:08:07,482
I know I'm meant to be loyal and obedient,
but the King doesn't know the facts.

102
00:08:07,553 --> 00:08:10,647
Once he does, he'll return home.

103
00:08:10,723 --> 00:08:13,089
And this is no time
for you to go back to the Holy Land.

104
00:08:15,127 --> 00:08:17,095
We're agreed, then.

105
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:22,961
Absolutely.

106
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:33,369
Would you like to say a few words?

107
00:08:41,754 --> 00:08:43,722
Go on, then.

108
00:08:46,626 --> 00:08:48,594
Will you marry me?

109
00:08:50,363 --> 00:08:52,331
What?

110
00:08:58,504 --> 00:09:02,702
The first time I held my bow, I knew.

111
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:08,708
It felt right, like it had been made for me,

112
00:09:11,117 --> 00:09:13,244
and that's how I feel about you.

113
00:09:15,988 --> 00:09:19,253
I promised your father
I would protect and look after you.

114
00:09:22,762 --> 00:09:24,730
But it works both ways.

115
00:09:26,098 --> 00:09:29,329
I look after my bow
because it protects and looks after me.

116
00:09:33,539 --> 00:09:35,507
Together, we're stronger.

117
00:09:43,182 --> 00:09:45,150
So...

118
00:09:54,393 --> 00:09:57,851
Marian, will you marry me?

119
00:10:03,336 --> 00:10:05,702
Robin, of every single man in the world,

120
00:10:07,406 --> 00:10:09,067
you are the only one...

121
00:10:09,141 --> 00:10:11,609
- Is that a yes?
- Will you let me finish?

122
00:10:11,711 --> 00:10:13,679
I'm sorry.

123
00:10:13,746 --> 00:10:20,948
The only one who would
propose over a fresh grave

124
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,181
by comparing me to your weapon.

125
00:10:23,255 --> 00:10:26,383
- Well, is that a no?
- Who'll give me away?

126
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:29,523
I can ask the King.

127
00:10:32,398 --> 00:10:35,458
So, we find Lardner and bring the King home,

128
00:10:37,103 --> 00:10:39,071
defeat the Sheriff

129
00:10:40,106 --> 00:10:42,074
and get married.

130
00:10:46,412 --> 00:10:48,573
Well, that sounds like a plan.

131
00:10:58,524 --> 00:11:02,255
Move, you worm!
Water! Water!

132
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:06,298
Right...

133
00:11:06,966 --> 00:11:08,558
Oh!

134
00:11:08,634 --> 00:11:10,727
What's that?!

135
00:11:10,803 --> 00:11:15,069
- It's a dove.
- Well, I've seen it all now.

136
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:21,270
Here, why do you keep a bird in your pocket?

137
00:11:21,347 --> 00:11:23,542
Why do you keep a worm in your pants?

138
00:11:26,018 --> 00:11:28,282
You should keep an eye on that belt of yours.

139
00:11:37,430 --> 00:11:40,661
- Where is he?
- Er... he's dead.

140
00:11:40,733 --> 00:11:44,999
- What about the message?
- Well, we got part of it, but Robin turned up.

141
00:11:45,104 --> 00:11:48,301
Turned up and got his message?!

142
00:11:49,208 --> 00:11:52,575
I don't know.
Look, he kind of took us by surprise.

143
00:11:53,813 --> 00:11:56,646
All we heard him say
was Lardner. That was it.

144
00:11:57,917 --> 00:12:00,112
Lardner. That's what he said.

145
00:12:05,324 --> 00:12:07,292
I blame you for this!

146
00:12:08,394 --> 00:12:11,522
You had him on your doorstep
and you let him escape!

147
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:13,758
You and your imbeciles!

148
00:12:13,833 --> 00:12:16,495
Six legs against one,

149
00:12:16,569 --> 00:12:19,163
and the one got away!

150
00:12:24,610 --> 00:12:26,510
That's the same pigeon.

151
00:12:26,579 --> 00:12:29,013
That was the same one
the jester brought, weren't it?

152
00:12:29,115 --> 00:12:31,083
That's impossible.

153
00:12:33,119 --> 00:12:35,087
There's an explanation.

154
00:12:35,154 --> 00:12:38,021
There is always a rational explanation.

155
00:12:41,794 --> 00:12:44,729
We know McLellan
and the Fool spoke together,

156
00:12:44,797 --> 00:12:47,322
so something was said
about the ring of Lardner.

157
00:12:47,399 --> 00:12:49,526
Maybe it's got
some kind of coded inscription on it.

158
00:12:49,602 --> 00:12:53,595
That's no problem. We'll just
torture the Fool until he tells us.

159
00:12:55,307 --> 00:12:57,332
He's disappeared!

160
00:12:57,409 --> 00:12:59,900
Disappeared?

161
00:12:59,979 --> 00:13:01,947
I know that, you blithering oaf!

162
00:13:16,162 --> 00:13:18,130
Know anyone called Lardner?

163
00:13:19,865 --> 00:13:23,164
Have you ever known or heard of anyone

164
00:13:24,036 --> 00:13:26,527
or anything called Lardner?

165
00:13:29,241 --> 00:13:32,176
Lardner's ring, what is it? What is it?

166
00:13:36,949 --> 00:13:42,216
Tell us where it is
or we will take every ring in this village.

167
00:13:44,323 --> 00:13:47,486
- It must be some kind of ring tax.
- Allan's with them.

168
00:13:51,230 --> 00:13:54,290
Leave me alone! I don't know anything!

169
00:13:57,369 --> 00:13:59,132
- Right, you've had your chance.
- Oh!

170
00:13:59,205 --> 00:14:01,537
Lose the ring or lose the finger.

171
00:14:02,474 --> 00:14:05,807
Somebody knows where Lardner is.

172
00:14:05,911 --> 00:14:07,708
Did he say Lardner?

173
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:11,113
- Does that mean something to you?
- I don't know anything!

174
00:14:11,784 --> 00:14:14,719
When the sand runs out,

175
00:14:14,787 --> 00:14:17,085
the finger comes off.

176
00:14:20,526 --> 00:14:23,359
Much, when we were in the Holy Land,

177
00:14:23,429 --> 00:14:25,420
do you remember anybody called Lardner?

178
00:14:25,531 --> 00:14:28,091
- No. Why?
- Because I have to find him.

179
00:14:30,469 --> 00:14:33,131
Where's er... Where's Will and Djaq?

180
00:14:33,205 --> 00:14:35,435
They said they've gone to get some honey.

181
00:14:36,008 --> 00:14:39,705
- They'll be back soon.
- If that's what they've gone for.

182
00:14:39,778 --> 00:14:41,712
Why does it need two of them? Hmm?

183
00:14:42,548 --> 00:14:44,516
Honey is one of those things.

184
00:14:45,651 --> 00:14:46,709
What things?

185
00:14:46,785 --> 00:14:50,516
What do you call it when you use a sweet,
innocent word but you mean something else?

186
00:14:50,589 --> 00:14:52,216
- A lie.
- No.

187
00:14:52,324 --> 00:14:55,088
- A euphemism.
- Euphemism.

188
00:14:55,160 --> 00:14:57,128
Exactly.

189
00:14:57,196 --> 00:15:00,757
Yes. There's a bit too much honey going on
round here if you ask me.

190
00:15:01,533 --> 00:15:06,163
Will and Djaq nipping off together, you
two going for long walks in the forest...

191
00:15:06,238 --> 00:15:08,172
He's jealous.

192
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:11,641
What happened to fighting for justice
and rebelling against the Sheriff?

193
00:15:11,744 --> 00:15:13,712
We should be sticking together.

194
00:15:14,446 --> 00:15:16,937
Instead, we're splitting into couples.

195
00:15:17,016 --> 00:15:18,950
To be perfectly honest,

196
00:15:19,785 --> 00:15:21,753
I don't fancy mine much.

197
00:15:23,822 --> 00:15:26,347
While you've been off gallivanting,
we've had to...

198
00:15:26,425 --> 00:15:29,724
- We haven't been gallivanting.
- I know what you've been up to.

199
00:15:29,795 --> 00:15:32,423
We've been burying a corpse in the forest.

200
00:15:35,434 --> 00:15:37,402
Is that a euphemism?

201
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:43,938
Speak know and spare this woman's finger.

202
00:15:44,009 --> 00:15:48,241
Say nothing, your silence proves your guilt.

203
00:15:48,347 --> 00:15:50,315
- Can we risk it?
- Yeah, we have to.

204
00:15:51,216 --> 00:15:54,117
I'll take them the long way round,
lead them away from camp.

205
00:15:54,954 --> 00:15:56,512
Stop!

206
00:15:58,324 --> 00:16:01,293
I know who Lardner is,
and I know about his ring.

207
00:16:02,428 --> 00:16:04,794
- Let the woman go.
- You're in Robin Hood's gang.

208
00:16:04,863 --> 00:16:06,831
Let the woman go!

209
00:16:11,003 --> 00:16:13,733
Now, tell me about Lardner.

210
00:16:15,274 --> 00:16:17,242
You'll have to catch us first!

211
00:16:17,343 --> 00:16:18,970
Get after them!

212
00:16:24,383 --> 00:16:26,351
Come on!

213
00:17:06,291 --> 00:17:08,225
That way!

214
00:17:17,836 --> 00:17:19,895
Whoa... Whoa... Whoa there, horsey.

215
00:17:20,439 --> 00:17:22,407
This is my lucky day, this is.

216
00:17:26,945 --> 00:17:28,913
Or it was.

217
00:17:32,885 --> 00:17:34,853
Robin!

218
00:17:34,953 --> 00:17:36,921
- What's happened?
- It's Gisborne.

219
00:17:36,989 --> 00:17:39,856
- He's in Locksley, terrorizing the people.
- What?

220
00:17:39,958 --> 00:17:41,926
He's searching for Lardner's ring.

221
00:17:44,663 --> 00:17:48,827
Ah, Gisborne the gullible.
I've got something to show you.

222
00:17:48,934 --> 00:17:51,528
First, you see it. Then you don't.

223
00:17:51,603 --> 00:17:53,764
There it is and there it isn't.

224
00:17:53,839 --> 00:17:55,807
What's your point?

225
00:17:58,644 --> 00:18:00,612
- How did you do that?
- Ah...

226
00:18:01,246 --> 00:18:03,237
You simply pull up on the handle here.

227
00:18:03,348 --> 00:18:05,316
The claw grabs hold of the bird.

228
00:18:05,384 --> 00:18:07,579
You lift up the lid, the bird's gone.

229
00:18:10,255 --> 00:18:12,348
The cooked bird has been hollowed out.

230
00:18:12,424 --> 00:18:16,258
You put a live dove inside it
and then la-di-da-di-da,

231
00:18:16,361 --> 00:18:18,522
plate full of birds' wings.

232
00:18:18,597 --> 00:18:20,565
Your fool, he's no sorcerer.

233
00:18:20,632 --> 00:18:23,567
He's no soothsayer
and he cannot predict the future.

234
00:18:23,635 --> 00:18:25,933
He was right about Lardner's ring though.

235
00:18:26,004 --> 00:18:27,972
Bring them in.

236
00:18:38,250 --> 00:18:40,810
Back home, my uncle had a friend,

237
00:18:40,886 --> 00:18:43,252
the Sultan's official pigeon handler.

238
00:18:43,355 --> 00:18:45,755
Wait, he handles pigeons?

239
00:18:45,824 --> 00:18:47,655
Yes, he handles pigeons.

240
00:18:47,726 --> 00:18:49,353
Job, I suppose...

241
00:18:49,428 --> 00:18:52,363
It's a very important job, Much.

242
00:18:53,699 --> 00:18:57,760
Now, the Sultan's very best pigeon,

243
00:18:57,836 --> 00:19:01,272
the smallest and quickest one

244
00:19:01,907 --> 00:19:05,274
was a pigeon called La Denah.

245
00:19:06,945 --> 00:19:08,913
Right.

246
00:19:09,314 --> 00:19:11,282
Right, does he have a ring?

247
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:14,183
And if he does, what's so special about it?

248
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:17,580
The Saracens use pigeons to carry messages.

249
00:19:17,689 --> 00:19:21,455
They put the message on a little ring tied
to the bird's foot and then let it fly off.

250
00:19:21,527 --> 00:19:24,792
It doesn't matter how far away the bird is
when you let it go,

251
00:19:24,897 --> 00:19:27,297
because it will always find its way home,

252
00:19:27,366 --> 00:19:29,334
carrying the message with it.

253
00:19:29,968 --> 00:19:34,200
But it isn't their home they're
searching for, it is their mate.

254
00:19:35,507 --> 00:19:38,908
All you have to do is
split the two of them up

255
00:19:39,811 --> 00:19:44,111
and a pigeon will carry a message
to the ends of the earth

256
00:19:44,183 --> 00:19:46,981
faster and straighter
than any man ever could,

257
00:19:47,819 --> 00:19:50,379
just to be with the one they love.

258
00:19:51,957 --> 00:19:56,724
So we could get a message to King Richard,
halfway across the world in no time at all?

259
00:19:56,795 --> 00:19:58,387
A matter of days.

260
00:19:59,998 --> 00:20:01,966
That's incredible!

261
00:20:02,034 --> 00:20:08,803
My question is why is the Sultan's
best pigeon being used by King Richard?

262
00:20:08,907 --> 00:20:11,705
Well, maybe they formed an alliance
or something.

263
00:20:11,777 --> 00:20:15,941
Or maybe King Richard and his army
have helped themselves.

264
00:20:17,683 --> 00:20:21,175
If they've got La Denah,
they've got the Sultan.

265
00:20:23,121 --> 00:20:27,080
And if they've got the Sultan,
they've got everyone.

266
00:20:34,533 --> 00:20:36,501
We have to go back to the tree.

267
00:20:37,302 --> 00:20:42,103
So that's what he had in his box, and the only
place he could have left it is up that tree.

268
00:20:42,174 --> 00:20:45,541
You make me sick, you know that,
betraying your old friends.

269
00:20:45,611 --> 00:20:48,045
Well, I hope you're enjoying it, Allan.

270
00:20:48,113 --> 00:20:51,844
- I couldn't live with myself.
- You won't have to for much longer, you see.

271
00:20:51,917 --> 00:20:55,853
The penalty for being in Robin Hood's gang
is death!

272
00:20:56,588 --> 00:20:59,455
Right, well, I'm glad
I could have been of service.

273
00:20:59,524 --> 00:21:04,552
- I think my work here is done. Could I go now?
- Of course, with him, to the gallows.

274
00:21:05,163 --> 00:21:09,600
Hang them both!
And feed their carcasses to the birds.

275
00:21:10,569 --> 00:21:12,901
Come along, Gisborne. No time to lose.

276
00:21:13,772 --> 00:21:16,673
- No, please! Don't let them do this to me!
- Hey...

277
00:21:20,345 --> 00:21:23,678
I do comedy. I don't do tragedy. Please?

278
00:21:23,749 --> 00:21:26,343
Well, seeing you swing will be comedy,
won't it?

279
00:21:26,418 --> 00:21:28,386
- Oh!
- Very good.

280
00:21:37,129 --> 00:21:40,292
If Robin gets the bird,
it means the King knows our plans,

281
00:21:41,133 --> 00:21:45,092
and the last thing we need is the King
and his army coming back to England

282
00:21:45,170 --> 00:21:47,638
before we're ready for them.

283
00:21:47,706 --> 00:21:51,267
So, we must catch the pigeon.

284
00:21:51,343 --> 00:21:53,971
Catch the pigeon now!

285
00:22:01,219 --> 00:22:03,585
But if we wait for Will,
then we can all go together.

286
00:22:03,689 --> 00:22:05,657
No. We have to go now. Come on!

287
00:22:05,724 --> 00:22:07,521
- And if Allan returns?
- Yes!

288
00:22:07,592 --> 00:22:11,050
If they knew what Lardner was,
they wouldn't have terrorized the village.

289
00:22:11,129 --> 00:22:14,587
We won't be long. Work on the message.
Give me the rope.

290
00:22:14,666 --> 00:22:16,634
Good luck.

291
00:22:46,798 --> 00:22:50,290
Do you think a bird could fly that distance
and still find its way home?

292
00:22:50,369 --> 00:22:52,599
The dove found its way back to Noah's Ark.

293
00:22:55,507 --> 00:22:57,475
How do they do it?

294
00:22:57,542 --> 00:22:59,510
They just follow their hearts.

295
00:23:05,350 --> 00:23:07,682
Won't be long.

296
00:23:07,753 --> 00:23:09,721
You take your time. No rush.

297
00:23:11,189 --> 00:23:13,953
Do you think that contraption's
strong enough to hold us both?

298
00:23:14,025 --> 00:23:16,084
Last a lifetime, that will.

299
00:23:16,161 --> 00:23:18,527
At least, your lifetime.

300
00:23:20,365 --> 00:23:22,060
I could do your job.

301
00:23:22,901 --> 00:23:24,869
I couldn't do yours.

302
00:23:48,927 --> 00:23:51,088
Can you see it?

303
00:23:52,097 --> 00:23:54,065
That's it, I think.

304
00:23:56,635 --> 00:23:59,798
Well... can you reach it?

305
00:23:59,905 --> 00:24:01,873
I'm nearly there.

306
00:24:07,312 --> 00:24:10,748
Robin! Robin, the Sheriff's coming!

307
00:24:16,521 --> 00:24:18,489
And Gisborne's with him!

308
00:24:22,394 --> 00:24:24,555
Run! Go without me!

309
00:24:24,629 --> 00:24:26,620
I'll hold them off.

310
00:24:30,435 --> 00:24:32,403
Hang on. Stop.

311
00:24:33,038 --> 00:24:35,006
Yeah, that's the tree.

312
00:24:50,355 --> 00:24:53,483
You're surrounded, Hood!
You're going nowhere!

313
00:24:53,558 --> 00:24:56,618
Get back on your horse
and get reinforcements!

314
00:25:00,665 --> 00:25:03,099
- What are you doing here?
- Hiding.

315
00:25:04,369 --> 00:25:05,768
Great.

316
00:25:07,105 --> 00:25:10,165
He could just wait until it's dark
and then release the bird.

317
00:25:10,242 --> 00:25:12,210
They'll be down before nightfall.

318
00:25:14,513 --> 00:25:17,607
- Come home.
- No. It needs... It needs more immediacy.

319
00:25:17,716 --> 00:25:19,980
- Come home now.
- Come home immediately.

320
00:25:20,051 --> 00:25:22,019
What's the matter with now?

321
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:24,748
- Immediately's better.
- Immediately's longer.

322
00:25:24,823 --> 00:25:27,189
- What?
- Lardner's a pigeon, not an eagle.

323
00:25:27,259 --> 00:25:30,626
- More words, more weight.
- It's one word, same as now!

324
00:25:30,729 --> 00:25:33,721
- Well, then, use now!
- Too many cooks!

325
00:25:35,433 --> 00:25:40,302
You know, traditionally, men in your position
offer men in our position a last request.

326
00:25:40,372 --> 00:25:42,306
I don't hold with tradition.

327
00:25:42,374 --> 00:25:46,208
It's just a little thing.
Would you scratch my nose? I've got an itch.

328
00:25:46,311 --> 00:25:48,472
Let the devil scratch it.

329
00:25:48,547 --> 00:25:50,708
You'll see him soon enough.

330
00:25:51,283 --> 00:25:53,251
Sir Guy's got Robin Hood trapped up a tree!

331
00:25:53,318 --> 00:25:55,616
Wants every available man down there now!

332
00:25:56,421 --> 00:25:58,889
- Get your horses! Quick!
- One last chance.

333
00:25:58,957 --> 00:26:02,222
While no one's looking, let us go.
We'll say no more about it, yeah?

334
00:26:04,262 --> 00:26:07,891
I'm a performer! I can't
die without an audience!

335
00:26:07,966 --> 00:26:09,934
It's an insult to my profession.

336
00:26:11,570 --> 00:26:13,936
I told you to keep an eye on that belt.

337
00:26:16,541 --> 00:26:21,001
Marian, they don't know you're up here.
If I make a run for it, they'll follow me.

338
00:26:21,112 --> 00:26:24,081
- You can escape with Lardner.
- They're coming back.

339
00:26:46,004 --> 00:26:47,972
That is a complete waste of arrows.

340
00:26:48,039 --> 00:26:52,339
- I've just given Allan something to think about.
- Think about us, running out of arrows!

341
00:26:52,410 --> 00:26:54,378
Look. Look, they're retreating.

342
00:26:58,984 --> 00:27:00,576
I have an idea.

343
00:27:00,652 --> 00:27:03,086
Do not let them escape while I'm gone.

344
00:27:03,154 --> 00:27:06,555
- They'll be dead by the time you get back.
- I won't hold my breath.

345
00:27:12,197 --> 00:27:14,165
Maybe you could use me as a hostage.

346
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:17,960
- What?
- You know Gisborne's feelings for me.

347
00:27:18,036 --> 00:27:20,630
He might let you go
if he thought it meant getting me back.

348
00:27:20,705 --> 00:27:22,229
Are you mad?

349
00:27:22,307 --> 00:27:25,606
I could say I was on my way home to him
and you ambushed me in the forest.

350
00:27:25,677 --> 00:27:27,645
He'd never fall for it.

351
00:27:27,712 --> 00:27:31,148
And how would that look?
"Robin Hood takes Lady Marian hostage. "

352
00:27:31,249 --> 00:27:35,777
Oh, yes. Us dying together might look
better, but it won't bring the King home.

353
00:27:35,854 --> 00:27:37,822
Even if Gisborne fell for
it, the Sheriff wouldn't.

354
00:27:37,889 --> 00:27:41,086
The Sheriff's gone. Gisborne's in charge.

355
00:27:41,159 --> 00:27:45,061
- Look, Robin, this could be our last chance.
- No! It's not happening.

356
00:27:45,130 --> 00:27:48,531
Robin, have you got a better idea?

357
00:27:51,169 --> 00:27:57,267
Right, "Your Majesty, no reinforcements.
John plans to steal your throne... "

358
00:27:57,342 --> 00:28:00,834
Plots. Plots is a better word.

359
00:28:02,313 --> 00:28:04,281
Plots is better.

360
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:10,485
"Your Majesty, no reinforcements.
John plots to steal your throne.

361
00:28:11,456 --> 00:28:13,981
"Come home now. "

362
00:28:15,627 --> 00:28:18,027
Short, sharp and to the point.

363
00:28:18,096 --> 00:28:21,088
I'll add, "Make peace," before "come home. "

364
00:28:21,166 --> 00:28:24,135
- Yeah.
- "Make peace immediately. "

365
00:28:24,235 --> 00:28:28,069
Ah... Well, you know, I do like immediately.

366
00:28:28,139 --> 00:28:30,107
But it needs more emotion.

367
00:28:30,175 --> 00:28:32,871
Make it bigger, raise
the stakes and alliterate.

368
00:28:32,944 --> 00:28:35,504
Alliteration makes it much more memorable.

369
00:28:35,580 --> 00:28:39,072
- Where have you come from?
- I appear, I disappear...

370
00:28:39,150 --> 00:28:43,450
It's what I do.
To tell how would spoil the illusion.

371
00:28:43,521 --> 00:28:44,818
He's with me.

372
00:28:45,690 --> 00:28:47,658
I thought you'd been caught.

373
00:28:47,726 --> 00:28:52,322
Madam, manacles are mere mist
to myself and my... my mate.

374
00:28:53,264 --> 00:28:56,256
- You know about the pigeon?
- Robin and Marian have gone to get him.

375
00:28:56,334 --> 00:28:58,825
Yeah, well, so have the Sheriff and Gisborne.

376
00:28:58,903 --> 00:29:01,531
They've got them trapped
up a tree, surrounded.

377
00:29:01,639 --> 00:29:03,607
We need to go. Now.

378
00:29:03,675 --> 00:29:05,643
Immediately!

379
00:29:08,446 --> 00:29:10,414
OK, line up!

380
00:29:12,450 --> 00:29:15,248
You, over there! Let's get him down!

381
00:29:16,121 --> 00:29:18,089
Line up!

382
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:45,415
Saddle me up another horse.
Make it a fast one.

383
00:29:45,483 --> 00:29:47,451
I'll be leaving shortly.

384
00:29:53,091 --> 00:29:55,719
It's a matter of time, Hood.
Say your prayers.

385
00:29:55,794 --> 00:29:58,661
If they come any nearer,
they'll get the same,

386
00:29:58,730 --> 00:30:00,459
and I won't be aiming at their feet!

387
00:30:02,700 --> 00:30:04,930
- Give me your arrows.
- It's not enough.

388
00:30:05,937 --> 00:30:07,905
They don't know that, do they?

389
00:30:09,140 --> 00:30:11,108
Ready?
Yeah!

390
00:30:11,176 --> 00:30:13,144
Move!

391
00:30:18,383 --> 00:30:20,908
I'll scream.
Then I can say you were holding me hostage.

392
00:30:20,985 --> 00:30:23,715
You didn't scream earlier. He's not stupid.

393
00:30:23,788 --> 00:30:25,881
I'll deal with this, Marian.

394
00:30:28,893 --> 00:30:31,259
It'll take them hours to cut down this tree,

395
00:30:31,329 --> 00:30:33,957
and in that time,
the gang will realize something's wrong.

396
00:30:34,032 --> 00:30:36,125
Yeah? They'll come and help.

397
00:30:36,201 --> 00:30:38,931
Come. Come. That's it, stop.

398
00:30:41,873 --> 00:30:43,841
I can't hear an axe.

399
00:30:43,908 --> 00:30:45,876
What are they waiting for?

400
00:30:52,951 --> 00:30:54,919
You know what that is, don't you?

401
00:30:54,986 --> 00:30:57,386
It's pitch.

402
00:30:57,488 --> 00:30:59,456
It burns hot and it burns fast.

403
00:31:00,024 --> 00:31:01,992
OK.

404
00:31:03,761 --> 00:31:05,729
Go! Go!

405
00:31:12,503 --> 00:31:14,494
- Much, I think it's this way.
- You think?

406
00:31:14,572 --> 00:31:16,870
- Shh!
- Don't tell me to shush!

407
00:31:16,941 --> 00:31:19,967
- We're looking for a tree in a forest!
- Shut up and listen!

408
00:31:21,913 --> 00:31:25,679
- I hope you're ready, Hood.
- I'm sensing it's that way.

409
00:31:29,621 --> 00:31:32,886
You tried to cover me in pitch once, Hood.
Do you remember?

410
00:31:35,226 --> 00:31:37,524
Tried to cook me alive
in my own armor.

411
00:31:37,595 --> 00:31:40,758
Yeah.
I should have gone through with it.

412
00:31:40,832 --> 00:31:44,131
Talking trees are good,
but burning bushes are better.

413
00:31:46,938 --> 00:31:49,907
I don't see a trough of water
for you to jump into.

414
00:31:49,974 --> 00:31:53,569
If I were bound and gagged, I couldn't
scream, so all we have to do...

415
00:31:53,678 --> 00:31:55,873
- No.
- There's no other way.

416
00:32:02,887 --> 00:32:05,287
- There's always another way.
- Shields!

417
00:32:06,491 --> 00:32:08,755
This is just putting off the inevitable.

418
00:32:08,826 --> 00:32:11,989
- You're not going back to him.
- I can do more good in the castle.

419
00:32:12,096 --> 00:32:15,190
Marian, I would rather die here
than have you go back...

420
00:32:15,300 --> 00:32:18,098
If you die now, my father died for nothing!

421
00:32:20,305 --> 00:32:22,273
Trust me.

422
00:32:22,340 --> 00:32:26,299
- I'll get us out of this.
- No, you won't, not without my help.

423
00:32:26,377 --> 00:32:28,345
Look, we're in this together.

424
00:32:29,514 --> 00:32:31,573
Now, tie me up.

425
00:32:31,683 --> 00:32:35,744
Right, listen to me.
He's only got two choices now, run or burn.

426
00:32:35,820 --> 00:32:38,288
Either way, he's finished.

427
00:32:38,356 --> 00:32:41,325
And any bird that leaves those leaves
dies in the sky.

428
00:32:42,527 --> 00:32:46,725
There are too many of them.
What are we going to do?

429
00:32:46,798 --> 00:32:48,766
After three, open the shield wall.

430
00:32:49,934 --> 00:32:51,902
One...

431
00:32:52,770 --> 00:32:54,738
Two...

432
00:32:59,110 --> 00:33:01,078
Three!

433
00:33:15,226 --> 00:33:19,686
Gisborne, if I die, Marian dies!

434
00:33:19,764 --> 00:33:21,891
No! Move!

435
00:33:21,966 --> 00:33:23,934
Marian!

436
00:33:27,572 --> 00:33:29,540
So it's true.

437
00:33:30,141 --> 00:33:32,109
You still love her.

438
00:33:32,176 --> 00:33:34,303
I've found your weakness, Gisborne.

439
00:33:34,379 --> 00:33:37,678
If you harm a single hair on her head,
I swear I will kill you.

440
00:33:38,616 --> 00:33:42,450
You will die more slowly and more painfully
than any man has ever died before.

441
00:33:42,520 --> 00:33:46,320
- I thought that was the point.
- No. It would have been quick and painless.

442
00:33:46,391 --> 00:33:51,294
You let me go, I'll leave
her behind, untouched.

443
00:33:53,831 --> 00:33:55,799
I'll think about it.

444
00:33:59,137 --> 00:34:01,105
Everybody back!

445
00:34:01,172 --> 00:34:03,538
Stay together!
Move!

446
00:34:03,608 --> 00:34:06,873
- Close up! Close up!
- Well, that bought us some time.

447
00:34:08,780 --> 00:34:09,872
Any ideas?

448
00:34:09,947 --> 00:34:11,915
We need to make Robin and Marian disappear.

449
00:34:11,983 --> 00:34:13,814
How do we do that?

450
00:34:14,752 --> 00:34:16,447
A puff of smoke.

451
00:34:18,222 --> 00:34:19,712
Careful.

452
00:34:19,791 --> 00:34:21,759
You all right?

453
00:34:22,593 --> 00:34:24,561
I won't leave you behind,

454
00:34:24,629 --> 00:34:26,597
but we can make him think that I will.

455
00:34:26,697 --> 00:34:30,258
So now we can all see the famous Robin Hood
for who he really is,

456
00:34:30,334 --> 00:34:34,498
a common coward,
using an innocent woman as a hostage!

457
00:34:34,572 --> 00:34:36,335
She's not an innocent!

458
00:34:37,108 --> 00:34:39,008
She's one of you lot,

459
00:34:39,110 --> 00:34:42,136
a pampered parasite,
living off the backs of the poor!

460
00:34:44,916 --> 00:34:46,349
It's nothing personal.

461
00:34:46,417 --> 00:34:50,319
You can't hide behind her skirts
forever. Come down and fight like a man.

462
00:34:50,388 --> 00:34:52,754
Remove your soldiers, Gisborne,

463
00:34:52,824 --> 00:34:55,850
or she dies at sundown!

464
00:34:57,295 --> 00:34:59,263
He wouldn't do that, would he?

465
00:34:59,330 --> 00:35:01,093
He's bluffing.

466
00:35:01,165 --> 00:35:03,463
This is a siege. All I have to do is wait.

467
00:35:04,368 --> 00:35:06,427
Well, you can't wait too long though.

468
00:35:06,504 --> 00:35:08,768
As soon as the Sheriff gets back,
she's gonna die.

469
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:17,338
Ready?

470
00:35:18,416 --> 00:35:20,384
Let's go.

471
00:35:49,113 --> 00:35:52,344
- The forest's on fire.
- It's a smoke screen.

472
00:35:52,416 --> 00:35:56,182
You, you and you, find the fire and
put it out! His men are in the forest.

473
00:35:57,021 --> 00:35:58,852
Right, surround the tree!

474
00:35:58,923 --> 00:36:00,948
Move!

475
00:36:01,025 --> 00:36:04,290
Make sure you can see
the man on either side of you at all times!

476
00:36:05,530 --> 00:36:07,464
Right, close in the circle. Move in!

477
00:36:15,606 --> 00:36:18,905
- Yes!
- I know what you're doing, and it won't work.

478
00:36:19,877 --> 00:36:22,311
We're ready for you,
ready and waiting.

479
00:36:26,184 --> 00:36:29,176
It's from Will. He's got a plan.

480
00:36:30,721 --> 00:36:32,120
Here, here.

481
00:36:34,559 --> 00:36:36,652
Tighten it.

482
00:36:40,198 --> 00:36:42,132
- OK, now, good.
- Horses.

483
00:36:45,970 --> 00:36:49,497
- You'll have to put your arms around me.
- No. I'm not coming.

484
00:36:49,574 --> 00:36:51,872
Marian, we haven't got time for this!

485
00:36:51,943 --> 00:36:55,310
- Then go, before they see the rope.
- We can both go.

486
00:36:55,379 --> 00:36:59,475
- Now.
- I'm staying here until you get away safely.

487
00:37:01,385 --> 00:37:03,649
- This is about Gisborne, isn't it?
- No.

488
00:37:04,889 --> 00:37:07,255
Inside us, we both know we belong together,

489
00:37:07,325 --> 00:37:12,126
and being apart will tear me apart,
but I'm more use in the castle.

490
00:37:12,196 --> 00:37:14,164
I can protect you better from there.

491
00:37:14,232 --> 00:37:16,200
It's our only hope.

492
00:37:17,935 --> 00:37:19,869
Trust me.

493
00:37:23,140 --> 00:37:25,108
- Give me your hand.
- No.

494
00:37:25,176 --> 00:37:27,872
- Just give me your hand.
- Is this a trick?

495
00:37:36,887 --> 00:37:38,855
It's an engagement ring.

496
00:37:46,230 --> 00:37:48,323
- This afternoon, we had a plan.
- Yes.

497
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:51,732
Find Lardner and bring the King home,

498
00:37:51,802 --> 00:37:54,896
defeat the Sheriff and get married,
in that order, Robin.

499
00:37:56,574 --> 00:38:00,305
First things first. This
isn't what I want to do.

500
00:38:01,279 --> 00:38:03,247
It's what I have to do.

501
00:38:06,484 --> 00:38:08,452
- I love you.
- I love you too.

502
00:38:36,781 --> 00:38:38,248
Where's Marian?

503
00:38:38,316 --> 00:38:41,342
- Let's get out of here. Has Will got everything?
- Yes.

504
00:38:41,419 --> 00:38:44,479
Help! Somebody, please help me!

505
00:38:44,555 --> 00:38:46,386
- Robin's gone!
- Get after him!

506
00:38:46,457 --> 00:38:48,891
Do not let him release that bird!

507
00:38:48,959 --> 00:38:51,120
£50 to the man who kills the pigeon!

508
00:38:51,195 --> 00:38:54,187
He's left me up here to die! Help me!

509
00:38:54,298 --> 00:38:56,266
Marian, I'm coming!

510
00:38:56,801 --> 00:38:59,292
- Is the note ready?
- Yeah. Where's Marian?

511
00:38:59,370 --> 00:39:04,398
She's fine. Just get out of here, and don't let
that bird go until you're out of range, right?

512
00:39:11,182 --> 00:39:12,740
Ah!

513
00:39:14,485 --> 00:39:16,453
I'm coming, Marian!

514
00:39:19,690 --> 00:39:22,716
I'll soon be there. Marian, hold on!

515
00:39:24,128 --> 00:39:26,096
Hurry up!

516
00:39:26,163 --> 00:39:28,131
Please hurry!

517
00:39:31,402 --> 00:39:33,370
It's all right. I've got you.

518
00:39:33,437 --> 00:39:35,405
I've got you.

519
00:39:38,042 --> 00:39:40,010
You're coming home with me.

520
00:39:42,613 --> 00:39:44,581
You're safe now.

521
00:39:50,521 --> 00:39:52,716
Release the bird! Release him!

522
00:40:21,152 --> 00:40:23,120
Come on!

523
00:40:28,125 --> 00:40:29,490
There!

524
00:40:37,101 --> 00:40:38,534
And fetch...

525
00:41:16,240 --> 00:41:18,435
- Cheer up.
- What have I got to be cheery about?

526
00:41:18,509 --> 00:41:19,976
Well, we've got a visitor.

527
00:41:21,645 --> 00:41:23,704
- Who are you?
- No, not him.

528
00:41:24,615 --> 00:41:27,675
- Him.
- It's Lardner.

529
00:41:27,751 --> 00:41:29,719
Say hello.

530
00:41:29,787 --> 00:41:31,755
You're joking?

531
00:41:32,623 --> 00:41:36,423
When you want something to disappear, the
trick is to divert the audience's attention.

532
00:41:36,527 --> 00:41:40,190
- We switched the birds.
- I happened to have a spare up my sleeve.

533
00:41:41,198 --> 00:41:43,598
We should have attached your note
to the decoy bird.

534
00:41:43,701 --> 00:41:45,396
It would have just weighed him down.

535
00:41:46,237 --> 00:41:51,470
Um... yeah, speaking of decoy birds,
is Marian all right with Gisborne?

536
00:41:51,542 --> 00:41:53,510
Yeah, she'll be fine.

537
00:41:54,612 --> 00:41:56,807
She can look after herself.

538
00:41:59,016 --> 00:42:02,508
Well, hey, do you want to do the honors?

539
00:42:02,586 --> 00:42:05,487
- At least we have some hope now.
- Yeah.

540
00:42:06,590 --> 00:42:08,558
Thanks to Lardner.

541
00:42:09,894 --> 00:42:11,862
Good luck, my friend.

542
00:42:11,929 --> 00:42:13,920
Safe journey home.

